    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 301113\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n413 AM MST Wed Sep 30 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- A gradual warming trend is anticipated through the remainder of\n  this week and into this weekend. Lower desert highs will return\n  to near normal across southcentral AZ and slightly above normal\n  for areas a long and west of the Lower Colorado River Valley. \n\n- Aside from lingering isolated shower activity in southern Gila\n  County today, dry conditions will return to the region through\n  early next week as high pressure takes hold over the region. \n  \n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /TODAY THROUGH FRIDAY/... \nLatest GOES satellite wv imagery and 500 mb streamline analysis \nreveals a deep cutoff low which is centered directly over Gila \nCounty. This low has been responsible for periods of showers and \nstorms across the Desert Southwest during the last 48 hours with \nsome locations receiving over 2.00\" of rainfall. A good chunk of \nsouthcentral AZ received around 0.25\"-1.00\" of rain with the \nhighest totals residing over northern and western Maricopa County \nand S Gila County. A few wrap around showers and storms are \ncurrently developing within the proximity of the upper-low and \nthis will likely continue through the rest of this morning and \ninto the early afternoon as the low center slowly migrates ewd. \nThese showers may produce an additional few hundredths to a tenth \nof an inch of rainfall. Dry air will gradual filter in behind this\nsystem as PWAT values fall below 1.00\" late this afternoon and \nevening, ultimately ending rain chances region-wide. Dry conditions\nwill prevail through the remainder of this week with essentially \nno rain chances expected across the entire Desert Southwest. \n\nDue to the presence of negative 500 mb hght anomalies and \nelevated soil moisture from yesterday's widespread rainfall, sfc \ntemperatures are expected to remain well below normal across \nsouthcentral AZ this afternoon. Lower desert highs will mainly \nrange from the low to mid 80s with temperatures across the higher \nelevations in the low to mid 70s. For areas along and west of the \nLower Colorado River Valley, clear skies and a drier airmass will\nallow temperatures to climb to around normal mainly in the low to\nmid 90s. Upper-lvl ridging will begin to build in behind the \ndeparting weather system Thursday and Friday, causing temperatures\nto steadily climb around 3-5 degrees each day. Lower desert highs\nshould return to the mid 90s to around 100 degrees by Friday.\n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/... \nModel guidance shows upper-lvl ridging migrating over the Four \nCorners region by this weekend with 500 mb hght fields increasing \nto around 588-590 dam. As this occurs, global models show a weak \nupper- low developing over N MX and far W TX which will induce \neasterly flow aloft over our forecast area. The increased pressure\ngradient between both features will bring breezy conditions to \nsoutheastern and southcentral AZ from Saturday through Monday. \nElevated winds will likely develop each morning and persist into \nthe early afternoon with gusts reaching 30-35 mph over the high \nterrain and 20- 25 mph across the lower deserts. This sfc easterly\nflow will help keep high temperatures near normal across \nsouthcentral AZ, but overnight lows will be elevated in the low to\nmid 70s. The 500 mb ridge axis looks to shift back to the over \nSoCal and the Great Basin early next week as a weak trough passes \nthrough the Intermountain West. This pattern should maintain \ntemperatures in a near normal range across central and eastern \nportions of the region, but allow highs to warm around 3-5 degrees\nabove normal across the western deserts where highs will likely \nreach the low 100s each afternoon.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 1111Z.\n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT:\nNo major weather issues will exist through tonight under SCT\nmidlevel cloud decks. Periodic 8-10K ft AGL cigs will continue to\nbe possible across the Phoenix metro this morning before\ndiminishing later in the afternoon. Winds will generally follow\ntypical diurnal trends, though some variability is likely during\ntransition periods.\n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH:\nNo weather concerns will exist through tonight under mostly clear\nskies. Confidence is excellent that winds will be variable for a\nprolonged period during the day with a tendency to become more out\nof the south starting this evening. \n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nAfter the last couple days of abundant wetting rainfall, drier \nconditions are expected to return today as a weather system \ndeparts the region. Temperatures will remain well below normal \nacross southcentral AZ and near normal for areas along and west \nof the Colorado River Valley through the rest of this week with \ngradual warming anticipated by this weekend. Relative humidity \nwill bottom out around 15-30% across the western districts and \nremain above 40% across the eastern districts today and Thursday. \nGood to excellent overnight recoveries can be expected through the\nremainder of this week. Winds will return to light and diurnal \npatterns with speeds remaining predominantly 10 mph or less over \nthe next few days before a stronger easterly gradient wind sets in\nby this weekend.\n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...None.\nCA...None.\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...Salerno\nLONG TERM...Salerno\nAVIATION...Kuhlman\nFIRE WEATHER...Salerno\n",
